const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'At what age can a widow or widower claim Social Security survivor benefits?',
    answer: 'A surviving spouse can claim Social Security survivor benefits as early as age 60 (or age 50 if disabled). If claimed at age 60, the survivor receives 71.5% of the deceased spouse’s benefit. Waiting until Full Retirement Age (age 67 for those born 1962 or later) yields 100% of the deceased spouse’s benefit.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I switch between survivor benefits and my own Social Security retirement benefit?',
    answer: 'Yes! This is one of the most powerful strategy rules for widowed seniors. You are permitted to claim survivor benefits as early as age 60 while letting your own personal retirement benefit grow until age 70 (earning 8% annual delayed retirement credits). At 70, if your own benefit is higher, you can switch to your own benefit.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does remarriage affect Social Security survivor benefits?',
    answer: 'If you remarry AFTER reaching age 60 (or age 50 if disabled), your remarriage does NOT affect your eligibility for survivor benefits on your deceased spouse’s record. However, if you remarry before age 60, you generally cannot receive survivor benefits as long as that marriage continues.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the earnings test apply to survivor benefits if I work?',
    answer: 'Yes. If you claim survivor benefits before reaching your Full Retirement Age (67) and continue working, the Social Security Earnings Test applies. In 2026, SSA withholds $1 for every $2 earned above the annual limit ($23,400) until the year you reach FRA.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the maximum Social Security survivor benefit amount?',
    answer: 'The maximum survivor benefit is 100% of the benefit the deceased spouse was receiving (or was entitled to receive at Full Retirement Age), including any delayed retirement credits the deceased earned prior to death.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a divorced widow/widower claim survivor benefits?',
    answer: 'Yes. A surviving divorced spouse can receive the exact same survivor benefit as a widow/widower if the marriage lasted at least 10 continuous years. Claiming ex-survivor benefits does not affect the benefits of any other survivors or current spouses.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is there a one-time Social Security death benefit for widows?',
    answer: 'Yes. Social Security pays a one-time lump-sum death payment of $255 to the surviving spouse who was living in the same household at the time of death, or to an eligible child.',
  },
];
