    <h2 id="explanation-heading" style="font-size: var(--text-2xl); color: #0D2137; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      Understanding Social Security Survivor Benefits
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      Social Security survivor benefits are designed to replace lost household income when a working spouse dies. Unlike standard retirement benefits which require waiting until age 62, a surviving spouse can begin receiving survivor benefits as early as age 60 (or age 50 if disabled).
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; line-height: 1.6;">
      The percentage of the deceased spouse's check you receive scales from <strong>71.5% at age 60 up to 100% at age 67 (FRA)</strong>. Crucially, widows and widowers retain the right to restrict their application to survivor benefits while allowing their own personal retirement benefit to accrue 8% annual delayed retirement credits until age 70.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 5 — REAL-LIFE SCENARIOS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
